        "action": "fill",
        "value": "I'm interested in Hudl because QA is close to the problem I keep returning to: deciding whether a system should be trusted, not just whether it runs. When I built a Playwright-based job-application agent in TypeScript, the most important part wasn't the automation but the validation ladder I designed around it, where a capability moved from UNIT_CONFIRMED to FIXTURE_CONFIRMED to LIVE_READ_ONLY_CONFIRMED to LIVE_MUTATION_CONFIRMED, because passing against a local fixture doesn't mean the thing works against reality. I did related work at Intelehealth, where I designed an evaluation framework for AI-generated clinical outputs over a 4,169-case benchmark and used stratified sampling to surface systematic failure modes rather than report a single aggregate score. Hudl builds software that real coaches and athletes depend on during a season, so failure modes have an immediate cost, and that's the kind of product I'd rather test than a demo with no users. I'd also want to be somewhere a junior engineer owns a real piece of the testing and tooling instead of isolated tickets, which is how Hudl describes trusting people to get work done their own way.",
